r J? J < Q — no. K > Q, A > K, 2 > A)\n- Or a bomb (four of a kind)\n- Or a rocket (but you don’t have jokers)\n\nYou **do** have a bomb: ♠3 ♥3 ♣3 ♦3 — four 3s.\n\nA bomb beats any non-rocket combination, including the current triplet + single.\n\nSo "bomb: ♠3 ♥3 ♣3 ♦3" is a valid move and beats the current play.\n\nBut should you use it now?\n\nConsider:\n- The bomb is very low-ranked (3s), so it’s unlikely anyone will beat it unless with a higher bomb or rocket.\n- The landlord played a relatively early mid-game combination (Queens + Jack). They may still have strong cards.\n- You are a peasant — your goal is to help your teammate win, not necessarily to win alone.\n- Your teammate still has 17 cards — likely many cards left.\n- You have 16 cards — similar count.\n\nUsing the bomb now stops the landlord and gives control potentially to your teammate.\n\nIf you don\'t bomb now, you cannot beat the play with any other legal move (no higher triplet + single), so you\'d have to pass.\n\nPassing lets the landlord continue their turn — they can play again, possibly running down their cards.\n\nBut by bombing, you interrupt them and force the next player (you) to play — no, turn order:\n\nYou are the **current player**, after the landlord (Next Player played).\n\nTurn order: Left (teammate) → You (you) → Right (landlord)\n\nWait — clarification:\n\n"Next Player (Right) is the Landlord" — so seating: You -> Landlord (Right) -> Teammate (Left)? No.\n\nStandard: turns go anticlockwise.\n\nIf Next Player (to your right) is the landlord, then order is:\n\nYou → Landlord (Right) → Teammate (Left) → back to You.\n\nBut the game history says: Turn 1 (Next Player): ♠J ♠Q ♣Q ♦Q\n\nSo the landlord (Next Player, to your right) just played.\n\nNow it\'s YOUR turn.\n\nAfter you, it goes to your teammate (Left).\n\nSo: Landlord played → Now You → Then Teammate\n\nIf you **pass**, then teammate can try to beat it — but do they have a bomb or higher triplet+single? Unknown.\n\nBut you kn