iplet K, A, or 2 (since K > Q, A > K, 2 > A)\n- Or a bomb\n- Or rocket (you don\'t have)\n\nDo you have triplet K? You have only one K → no.\n\nTriplet A? Only one A → no.\n\nTriplet 2? Only one 2 → no.\n\nSo no natural higher triplet + single.\n\nOnly option: **bomb** or **pass**.\n\nValid moves given: bomb or pass.\n\nBombing will beat the play and give you the lead — meaning after you play the bomb, it becomes **free play** — you get to start the next round of play.\n\nThat could be powerful — you can then lead a small card or sequence to help your teammate take control.\n\nBut is it worth using a bomb on Queens + Jack?\n\nPossibly — because:\n- The landlord may be trying to build momentum.\n- You don’t know what your teammate has — they might not have a bomb or strong play.\n- If you pass, and teammate cannot beat it, landlord continues and may run away with the game.\n- By bombing, you stop the landlord and gain initiative.\n\nAlso, your bomb is 3s — the lowest bomb — so it’s the least valuable bomb (since higher bombs are stronger and rarer). Using a low bomb early is often acceptable.\n\nMoreover, you have many low-to-mid cards — you might benefit from controlling the pace.\n\nAlternative: pass and hope teammate bombs or plays.\n\nBut if teammate passes too, landlord continues — could lead another hand — dangerous.\n\nGiven that you have a viable bomb and no other way to beat the play, and taking control could help coordinate with teammate, **it\'s better to bomb now**.\n\nReason: Using the bomb stops the landlord’s progress, gains you the lead to set the next play, and allows you to potentially offload singles or start sequences that your teammate can support. Since your bomb is low (3s), it\'s acceptable to use it early for control. Passing risks letting the landlord continue unchecked.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♠3", "♥3", "♣3", "♦3"]}\n\nFinal output:\n\nEvaluation: The landlord played a triplet of Queens with a single Jack, which I cannot beat with a higher 